      "text": "To find Bob's favorite number, we'll first follow the given constraints.\nFirst, we know that it is between 50 and 100, so we can narrow it down to this range.\nThen, it is a multiple of 11, so the number must end with a 4 or a 8.\nFinally, it is not a multiple of 2, so it can either be odd or a multiple of 11 with 1 added (but we aslo have to conertain it is odd)\nAnd in it's most other constraints it is a multiple of 3, so the sum of its digits is a multiple of 3.\nHence our number may be: 54, 74, 78. \nThe sum of the digit of these numbers gives: 10, 16 , 15. From this we know thats only 74 is a multiple of 3.\nSome quick checks show that all the other numbers meet all the uniqueities, but one thing is left: Check one last time that it is an odd number and not 2 as well. Yep, our solution is 74.\nWhich is our favorite number\n\nProblem 67\nEveryone at work is about to have lunch. The cost of the meal is $36.00. Each person is supposed to pay an equal amont and give $ 2\\over 3$ off the tab. How should the 2 people divide the make up of the $2\\over 3$ discount when they split it equally?\nI. Each person is to pay $10.04\nII. The 1st person pays $9.23 and the 2nd person pays $9.19\nIII. The 1st person pays $8.32 and the 2nd person pays $8.40\nEach person is to pay $\\frac 13$ off the tab, so that it equals $\\frac 23$, and they are dividing that $2\\frac 13$ equally. I multiplied $\\frac 13=\\frac 39$ by 9, to get $3$, then multiplied it back into $\\frac 23=9.23$, and got $\\frac 29$. I divided $\\frac29$ by 2 because 2 people, so I got $\\boxed{10.04}$ in a sense that you count one penny but will say you pay a larger portion than the penny-decimal amount.\nII: Same method as step 1 \nIII:Same method as I.\n\n\nProblem 68\nConvert: 48 deg, 58 min to radians. To convert degrees and minutes to radians, we first need to convert them to a single degree amount with decimals and then switch units from degrees and minutes.\n\n48 deg, 58 min is equal to 48 + $\\frac{58}{60}\\approx$ 49.93 degrees.\n\nTherefor: $\\frac{49.93}{360}\\pi$\n\nYou've already done $1$ and now you just need to keep messing around. :P\n\n\n~LibAi\n\n\"...How should this make up of the $2\\over 3$ discount be divided when they…\"\nmake up of the fraction of 2/3, which is the fraction $3/5$  of the $2\\over 3$ discount. \nIn order to compute this we just inverse the fraction of 2/3  i.e 3/5 and multiply it by 2/3, which gives the numerator part, numerator of the fraction is $3/5$ of 2/3, or 6/15 is 2/5 or 2/5 of the 2/3 discount and so on\nIsn't 48deg58min $\\approx 49.98$ degrees?\n\nThe previous answer changed n in my work.... Silly me....\nso,0.875,1 degree \n\nThe way I did #68:\n\nDivides the angle into eighth and its medicale unit, to make your stuff easy. in my work, I make myself adding $\\frac{1}{8}$ of the angle the degrees into the minutes\neg.   $30$ divided into it's $8 previously $\\approx$ $4$\n\nnow, we can do: the second part has the fraction $\\frac{30}{90}- \\frac{30}{144}$ =~ $0.624$ \nSo we get: \n$30\\times \\frac{624}{144} \\approx$ $125.2$\n\n====== => now, the fractions in the top are $125$ ~ $\\frac{2}{3}$ \nand those in the bottom are $8$ to get to make your work convenient.",
